{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/51643-in-need-of-some-advice-w-r-t-designing-a-contract","id":51643,"title":"In need of some advice w/r/t designing a Contract","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-01-17T08:51:48-08:00","id":51643,"title":"In need of some advice w/r/t designing a Contract","content":"I have been brainstorming ideas for a Contract with poison. It shows up in myths, fairy tales, and legends enough I feel to warrant an examination. Not to mention that poison itself could be described as fae: fickle, subtle, treacherous.<br /> <br /> Anyway, I thought I would share what I've come up with so far, in hopes of getting some feedback and advice and several other sets of eyes looking at the problem. I worry that my ideas might be a little too flat or bland for a set of changeling powers, and could use some sprucing up.<br /> <br /> So,<br /> <br /> 1: The changeling can sniff out the poison in a substance, be it in a cup of liquid, a Goblin Fruit, or in a person's body. This requires 1 point of Glamour and a Wits + Wyrd roll. On a success, the user learns whether there is anything poisonous about the subject. On an exceptional success she pinpoints what kind of poison it is, not scientifically-speaking but in general, commonly understood categories. I'm also wondering if that should just be part of the regular success, with the exceptional granting some other boon. I'm thinking the Catch for this Clause is something like &quot;The changeling is checking the contents of something that someone she genuinely cares about is going to consume.&quot;<br /> <br /> I should probably talk about definitions and terms here before getting too far in. For the purposes of this Contract, &quot;poison&quot; includes both traditional poisons (such as cyanide), as well as venoms and intoxicants. Anything that causes immediate or nearly-immediate harm when put into the body. The first Clause could be used, then, to determine if a certain snake is venomous, or if the heroin you're buying is legit.<br /> <br /> 2: The changeling can immunize himself to one kind of poison (or venom, or intoxicant...) until next sunrise or sunset. Again, like in the first Clause, choices are more poetic (or household) than scientific: cobra venom, arsenic, LSD, etc. At first I was going to make this Clause give the changeling immunity to all poisons, but then I remembered Houses of the Blooded, and I like the dilemma it raises, both for the changeling utilizing this Clause and for any would-be poisoners. I like the name &quot;The King's Dilemma&quot; for the Clause. You can only have one version of this Clause active at any time, unless you get an exceptional success on the roll, in which case you may choose a second immunity. I'm thinking 1 Glamour again. The roll is either Stamina + Wyrd (since it affects your bloodstream) or Composure + Wyrd (since the Clause involves &quot;keeping a straight face&quot; as it were), or Wyrd + Medicine. The Catch: I'm not sure, but I was thinking maybe the Clause is free of charge if the changeling activates it after intentionally or accidentally poisoning himself (so, eating a lethally toxic Goblin Fruit that looks almost identical to its healing cousin, or doing a shot of drain cleaner and then changing your mind about sloughing off this mortal coil just yet).<br /> <br /> 3: At this level, the changeling can speed up or slow down the poison coursing through someone's veins. It's a Wyrd + Medicine roll, and success lets one add or subtract up to one's Wyrd in increments. So, if a changeling with Wyrd 1 uses this on someone suffering from a poison that affects him once every hour, he can make it so it instead affects him once every two hours, or once every half an hour. Alternatively. Alternatively, I might flip the meaning of speed up and slow down, and each point of Wyrd lets the changeling double or halve the total time the poison remains in one's system. I kind of like that one better. Expel a brown recluse bite in one agonizing go before necrosis sets in, or keep someone opium'd out all week long. Not sure if this should be 1 or 2 Glamour. The Catch? <i>Allez savoir.</i><br /> <br /> 4: The changeling dips her finger into a cupful of liquid and alters its Toxicity rating up to her Wyrd. I'm thinking 2 Glamour, Wyrd + Science for this one. Great for changing water into wine (or, actually, water into something that looks, smells, and tastes like weak vodka but will still put a fire in your belly). On an exceptional success, or by expending a point of Willpower on a normal success, you can also change the appearance and taste. The transformation lasts for a scene, unless the beverage is drunk, in which case it has its affect and doesn't revert back in someone's innards (which is an issue I kept thinking of when reading the Matter spell Transmute Water). I'm thinking that this doesn't let a changeling alter the category of poison, unless she's turning a normally non-toxic substance into a toxic one (hence, water into wine, or its probably more popular contemporary, saline solution into pure heroin). As for the Catch, again, I'm uncertain. Maybe something about sprinkling powdered amethyst or pearl into the drink?<br /> <br /> 5: I'm at a loss here. What sort of poison-related ability would be on par with turning invisible, creating workable objects out of scrap, becoming a tree, or removing a solid object from a reflection? I've had a couple of scattered, disparate ideas:<br /> <br /> One is the crafting of a special poison, as an extended action wherein the changeling can choose special effects to add to it. Poisons that make the victim clumsy or cloud their mind. It feels a little un-fae though. Maybe too involved.<br /> <br /> Another idea I had was the ability to imbue another Clause into a liquid, so long as the Clause 1) affects either the self or one other target, and 2) is used towards harmful ends. So, imbuing a drink with Fickle Fate or The Lord's Dread Gaze, but also a use of Calling Forth Water in which the drinker becomes dangerously dehydrated as all the moisture in his body escapes through his skin. This feels horrendously out of place with the rest of the Contract, though. I feel like potion-brewing has a place in Changeling, but should probably be handled with either a Goblin Contract or maybe an Entitlement.<br /> <br /> I was also thinking of taking the phrase &quot;poison pen&quot; somewhat literally. The changeling mixes a dose of the poison with ink, and uses the mixture to write a message, or draw a picture, or compose a song, etc. The first person to read/see the piece is affected as if he had been injected with the poison in question. For a point of Willpower, the changeling can imbue a special instruction with regards to the poison's delivery; a venomous song might only deliver its bite to whoever plays it on a piano, or a letter's poison could be made to infect only a specific person (or one kind of person, i.e., &quot;the first Hunterheart to read this note&quot;). I personally like this idea the best, it's thematic, treacherous, a play on words, but I also don't know if it's powerful enough for a fifth-dot Clause. Four dots, sure, but maybe it needs some extra teeth before it can be called five.<br /> <br /> --<br /> <br /> In addition to those things, I've bee mulling over Affinity as well. I don't feel like poison use is as universal a faery ability as, say, blessing someone or shapechanging, so I don't see it as a universal Contract. My first thought is to make it affinity for Darklings, adding to their treacherous reputation, though I could also see an argument for Fairest or Wizened affinity (in a meta-game context, however, I'd lean more towards Darklings, since they only have two canon affinity Contracts, while Fairest and Wizened both have three).<br /> <br /> Finally, there's the name. &quot;Contracts of Poison&quot; sounds too bland, too straightforward. &quot;Contracts of Venom&quot; is a bit more euphonic, but also misleading. I briefly considered &quot;Contracts of Bottles&quot; before realizing that that didn't really convey what the magic was about. At the moment I've got a soft spot for <i>Contracts of Hemlock</i>, unless someone can think of a more fitting, poetic name.<br /> <br /> So, there you have it. Any advice, counsel, pointers, and/or bric-a-brack would be greatly appreciated.<br /> <br /> Thank you for taking the time to read this.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1423-espritdecalmar","name":"espritdecalmar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1423_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"941"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-17T10:12:54-08:00","id":51685,"title":"","content":"Poison and Venom are super cool, and a common trope in many fairytales. Good subject for a Contract!<br /> <br /> Some thoughts:<br /> <br /> 1 dot Clauses: In many cases, the first clause of a Contract involves magical &quot;perception&quot; of the subject of the Contract. Your choice of clause follows this principle pretty faithfully and the dice pool seems right to me. The catch seems okay to me, if a little specific for an entry clause into a Contract. I would suggest making the catch a little more attainable, such as &quot;The changeling tastes (but need not ingest) the contents of something she thinks may be poisoned.&quot;<br /> <br /> 2 dot Clauses: Two dot clauses often offer a resistance to the subject of the Contract, which this clause totally does. I'd say that Stamina + Wyrd is a good dice pool to activate the power. As for the catch, it should reward the character for acting like a fairy ... and since poison is an underhanded and treacherous discipline by its mythic nature, I would recommend something like &quot;The changeling uses this contract in attempt to convince another that something is not poisoned.&quot;<br /> <br /> 3 dot Clause: Three dot clauses manipulate in some way the subject of the Contract. If I were writing this contract, I think I would sorta combine the aspects of your 3 and 4 dot clause ... making it so the changeling is able to swap around the various aspects of one poison for another (Toxicity, duration, effects blah blah). Medicine + Wyrd would be a good dice pool, I think. The catch could be the quality being switched belongs to a poison the changeling has willingly ingested within the last 24 hours. This dovetails nicely with the catches from the previous clauses.<br /> <br /> 4 dot Clause: Summoning or creating the subject of the Clause is a common effect of most 4 dot Clauses. This could be your magical poisoning effect, giving the ability to poison a substance without any special ingredients or preparation. This poison, being magical, would be untraceable and indistinguishable from the substance that carries it. Magical perception would reveal it, tho. Larceny + Wyrd would be cool. A good catch could be something like &quot;The changeling prepares the poisoned food/drink/whatever in front of the intended victim.&quot;<br /> <br /> 5 dot Clause: As the pinnacle of the clause, this power should really pack some metaphysical punch. It should bestow upon the changeling the ephemeral and narrative power of the subject of the Contract. I like the idea of a Changeling having a &quot;venomous tongue&quot; and having the ability to poison others with words and deadly whispers. Maybe a changeling with this clause can damage his victims through poisoned lies and rumours. Expression + Wyrd - Composure, with a success inflicting the effects of some fairy dream poison on the target would be cool. The catch, following in the theme, would be &quot;Used against a target that implicitly loves or trusts the changeling&quot;<br /> <br /> <br /> Again, this is a super cool idea for a contract. I will keep an eye on this thread. I am excited to see what you do with it!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1996,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1996-firefly-night","user":"Firefly Night","date":". 01-17-2014, 01:35 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1996-firefly-night","name":"Firefly Night","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"150"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-17T19:35:20-08:00","id":52087,"title":"","content":"As is becoming extremely common for me, I strongly agree with <b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1996-firefly-night\">Firefly Night.</a></b><br /> My only tweaks would be making the 2 dot last one scene for simple success and full day for Exceptional. Plus, let the player stack as many protections as they want, if they are so paranoid that they are willing to burn a bunch of glamor on resisting many poisons, let them--then expect the ST to throw a need for other glamor use at them.<br /> Also I could see a willpower being needed in addition to glamor for the 4 and 5 dot levels.<br /> For 5 dots, how about giving the character a poisonous touch with toxicity = Wyrd?<br /> My only concern is why would I, as a player, want the defensive clauses when I can take the Iron Stamina (or is it Stomach?, I get those two confused) Merit? Maybe the defesive aspect of the contract should be granted to others rather than self, like resist at 2 dot and purge from system at 4.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I also post a long form story, based on a Changeling: The Lost campaign at: <a href=\"https://www.fanfiction.net/s/10018173/1/Great-Fight-A-Twilight-Tommy-Tale\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.fanfiction.net/s/1001817...ght-Tommy-Tale</a><br /> I hope you will enjoy it.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2020-gitariart","name":"GitariArt","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-18T21:33:36-08:00","id":52989,"title":"","content":"I feel like a Contract based solely around physical poison is a bit... how to say it... too focused, maybe? Changeling's contracts tend to be thematically grouped. I'd combine physical poison (drugs, toxins, what have you) with spite, just because the metaphor of &quot;poisoned the crowd against me&quot; and &quot;poisoned his thoughts&quot; is just too rich, and in Lost, metaphor is reality. I don't have specific clause-by-clause feedback, yet, but I'll see what I can do to put some together.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1168-hobbitguy1420","name":"HobbitGuy1420","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1168_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"332"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-25T12:26:21-08:00","id":57567,"title":"","content":"Thanks for the responses. I will see what I can come up with now.<br /> <br /> First, GitariArt, according to the rules updates in the back of Demon, the Iron Stamina merit negates fatigue- or injury-related penalties. There is, however, Hardy, for which each dot grants the holder an extra die to &quot;any rolls to resist disease, poison, deprivation, unconsciousness, or suffocation.&quot; So, outright immunity from a Clause is still a competitive advantage, I think, though I am intrigued by your suggestion to grant immunity to others. Hmm.<br /> <br /> Right now, I have these:<br /> <br /> <b>\u2022 Taste Testing</b><br /> The first boon a changeling receives from entering into Contract with hemlock is the ability to detect poisons. Every poison, venom, and drug has its own special scent, taste, feel. With this Clause, the harmful substances reveal themselves to her, like a noxiously-sweet perfume coming off a person's body or a discordant note in a glass of wine.<br /> <b>Cost:</b> 1 Glamour<br /> <b>Dice Pool:</b> Wits + Wyrd<br /> <b>Action:</b> Instant<br /> <b>Catch:</b> The changeling actually tastes (though not necessarily ingest) something she thinks to be poisoned. Taking a sip of wine or licking the sweat off a stranger's brow. Easy enough.<br /> <b>Roll Results:<br /> Dramatic Failure:</b> The changeling is unable to puzzle out the specific odors of poison. Furthermore, her senses become confused; for the rest of the scene she does not reroll 10s on perception rolls, and further takes a -1 penalty on perception rolls that specifically involve smell or taste.<br /> <b>Failure:</b> The changeling can't tell if a substance is poisonous or not. So sorry.<br /> <b>Success:</b> The changeling detects the presence of poison in something or someone, described through the medium of one of her five mortal senses. It should be noted that using this Clause on a living creature does not distinguish between whether they have been poisoned or if they themselves are poisonous in some way (so, using this Clause on Venombites can be tricky, at best). It should also be noted that, for the purposes of this Clause, intent at least half-matters. Wine is technically, according to the strictures of the Contracts of Hemlock, a poison, but a glass of wine won't normally be treated as such. As such, if this Clause is used on an intoxicant, the changeling learns if there is any other poison in the intoxicant besides the drug itself.<br /> <b>Exceptional Success:</b> The changeling not only detects the presence of poison, but receives a general idea of what kind of poison is at work (e.g., &quot;The king's swallowed mercury,&quot; or, &quot;There's LSD in the communion wine.&quot;)<br /> <br /> <b>\u2022\u2022 The King's Dilemma</b><br /> Rulers of old would often, over long periods of time, take small amounts of a poison to immunize themselves to it. This Clause lets a changeling do the same, confounding would-be assassins or letting him play assassin himself; see, this drink is perfectly safe. No need to be so paranoid. Of course, each use of The King's Dilemma only applies to a specific poison, hence the name. What must the emperor protect himself against today?<br /> <b>Cost:</b> 1 Glamour<br /> <b>Dice Pool:</b> Stamina + Wyrd<br /> <b>Action:</b> Instant<br /> <b>Catch:</b> The changeling activates this Clause just as he is about to ingest something poisonous to convince someone that it is not.<br /> <b>Roll Results:</b><br /> <b>Dramatic Failure:</b> Whatever poison the changeling wished to inure himself to is now treated as having a Toxicity rating one point higher if he is subject to it for the rest of the scene.<br /> <b>Failure:</b> The Clause fails to activate, and the changeling is no more immune than he was before.<br /> <b>Success:</b> The changeling chooses one kind of poison (such as arsenic, black widow venom, or morphine). For the rest of the scene, he is completely immune to the poison's effects.<br /> A changeling can activate this Clause multiple times if he wishes to have multiple immunities, though each activation attempt is made at a -1 penalty for every immunity already granted from this Clause.<br /> <b>Exceptional Success:</b> As above, but the immunity lasts until next sunrise or sunset, whichever comes first.<br /> <br /> I need to think more about the later Clauses, taking into account what y'all have said. The spite angle, HobbitGuy1420, is one which must be considered; its being thematically appropriate and all.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1423-espritdecalmar","name":"espritdecalmar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1423_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"941"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-25T17:14:38-08:00","id":57761,"title":"","content":"espritdecalmar,<br /> I admit I am not familiar with the Darmon rewrites. I also mentioned that I get Iron Stamina confused with Iron Stomach (obviously both from the core WoD book).<br /> I have now rreread iron Stomach and I feel that its claims that the character can inhest anything with no ill effect does cover poison. Add the merit natural Immunity and the character is pretty seriously resident.<br /> I see that there would still be gaps (ujected toxins possibly, for one example). I'm just suggesting that if I had a PC worried about potential poisoning, then I would quickly snap up the much cheaper XP cost of a 1 and 2 dot merit and then wind up with a relatively redundant clause when I did buy the contract you offer. Because I would absolutely get the contract, I just think it becomes another clause like so many others in the rules where I have to pay through it to get to something real useful at high dot levels.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I also post a long form story, based on a Changeling: The Lost campaign at: <a href=\"https://www.fanfiction.net/s/10018173/1/Great-Fight-A-Twilight-Tommy-Tale\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.fanfiction.net/s/1001817...ght-Tommy-Tale</a><br /> I hope you will enjoy it.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2020-gitariart","name":"GitariArt","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-25T19:58:20-08:00","id":57844,"title":"","content":"I agree with HobbitGuy, contracts are usually not so practical and straightforward. <br /> <br /> For one of the high dot levels of this contract, I was thinking about poison in fairy tales and myth: how can a poison contract not give you the ability to create a poison apple? In other words, you should be able to charm a piece of food to put someone into an enchanted sleep that is indistinguishable from death. Sleeping Beauty's encounter with a spinning wheel put her into a enchanted sleep that lasted a hundred years.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2002-quynce","name":"Quynce","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2002_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"34"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-26T09:57:31-08:00","id":58150,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Quynce</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/51643-in-need-of-some-advice-w-r-t-designing-a-contract?p=57844#post57844\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For one of the high dot levels of this contract, I was thinking about poison in fairy tales and myth: how can a poison contract not give you the ability to create a poison apple? In other words, you should be able to charm a piece of food to put someone into an enchanted sleep that is indistinguishable from death. Sleeping Beauty's encounter with a spinning wheel put her into a enchanted sleep that lasted a hundred years.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh man, you know, I completely passed over those. They're so obvious too. Gotta think about this.<br /> <br /> GitariArt: Are you thinking of the Toxin Immunity merit? I do not believe it exists anymore in GMC. Admittedly, I do not actually have access to the Core NWoD rulebook, so I might be mistaken about some things, but no such thing appears in the rules updates present in the back of Demon. As one of the higher-level Clauses in the Contract will involve altering Toxicity, I believe that could handle granting poison immunity to others, as per your suggestion. But I will ruminate on this further. I'm going to look at the Contracts of Elements, for one, and think about the way they are structured and what that means.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1423-espritdecalmar","name":"espritdecalmar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1423_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"941"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}